  "faqs": [
    { "q": "What is pre-commission cleaning, and why does a new system need it?", "a": "Pre-commission cleaning is the controlled flushing of a new or extended water system before it is put into use. New pipework carries installation debris such as swarf, flux, jointing compound and loose corrosion, all of which can foul valves, block strainers and shorten the life of pumps and heat exchangers. Cleaning the system first protects the equipment you have just paid to install and gives it the best possible start. We carry this out in line with BSRIA guidelines, including BSRIA BG29/2012." },
    { "q": "Will you need to shut my system down?", "a": "Usually not. In most cases we can carry out the flush without taking the wider system offline, so day-to-day use continues around the work. Where a short isolation is unavoidable we agree it with you in advance and keep it as brief as we can." },
    { "q": "Do I need a licence or consent to discharge the flushed water?", "a": "Yes. Flushing water cannot simply be sent to the sewer, a discharge consent must be in place before it is released, and this is a legal requirement rather than a formality. We can advise on what is needed and help arrange the consent as part of the works, so nothing is discharged before permission is granted." },
    { "q": "Does flushing use a lot of water?", "a": "It uses less than people often expect. Careful flushing to BSRIA guidelines is about method rather than sheer volume, and low-volume flushing techniques are available where water use or discharge needs to be kept down. We plan the approach around your site so that the system is cleaned thoroughly without wasting water." },
    { "q": "What does the chemical flushing actually remove?", "a": "The flush is designed to lift and carry away the corrosion products, fine debris and installation residues that settle in new pipework. We use a gentle chemical flush rather than an aggressive one, so the system is cleaned without putting undue stress on fittings and seals. The aim is clean, free-running pipework ready for the treatment or dosing regime that follows." },
    { "q": "Which guidelines do you follow for pre-commission cleaning?", "a": "We work to recognised BSRIA guidance for pre-commission cleaning of water systems, including BSRIA BG29/2012. Following an established method matters because it gives a consistent, defensible standard of cleaning rather than a one-off approach. It also means the work sits comfortably alongside any wider compliance regime you already have in place." },
    { "q": "How do I know the system is clean once flushing is finished?", "a": "Cleanliness is assessed against the criteria set out in the BSRIA guidance rather than judged by eye alone, and we record what was done as part of the process. That record gives you and any incoming maintenance contractor a clear starting point for the life of the system. If a further stage of flushing is sensible before sign-off, we will say so rather than rush to close the job." }
